// cell -- the constants both font paths speak.
//
// NAMES.md section 5: a number that means something is spoken by a named
// zero-action function, never as a bare literal at a call site. The return clause
// is free, so naming a constant costs nothing at all -- and a literal 32 sitting
// in txt_spr_run is indistinguishable from a literal 32 that happens to be a
// margin, which is the failure this prevents.
//
// Code points are expressed as (import txt_first) + n for the same reason: the digits
// and the minus sign are at fixed offsets from the font's first glyph, and
// writing them that way says WHICH table the number indexes.

// The advance: how far the pen moves per character, which for a monospaced face
// is also how many columns a glyph draws. It is the *width* only -- the default
// face is 8x16 now, so the row count is txt_cellh() and the two are different
// numbers. Everything that measures a string still asks this one; everything
// that measures a line of them had to be found and changed to ask the other.
txt_cell() {
  int cell = (import txt_st)[0];
} return int cell;

// The last one the loaded face can draw. 126 for the built-in table -- printable
// ASCII and nothing above it, because a byte over 126 is a UTF-8 continuation and
// this engine has no notion of one -- and 255 for a full code page loaded from a
// .psf, which is where the box-drawing and accented glyphs come from.
txt_last() {
  int first = (import txt_first);
  int facen = txt_facen();
  int last = first + facen - 1;
} return int last;
